WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.268 --> 00:00:06.810
SPEAKER: In this
demo, we'll show you

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:10.650
how to migrate a two-tier LAMP
stack application with Migrate

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.330
for Anthos.

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:16.410
Let's start with our VMware
vSphere setup on-premises.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.440
There are two VMware
VMs in this application,

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.750
one for the application
server and one

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:22.930
for the database server.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.470
This CRM application
is the application

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.630
that we are going
to be migrating.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:35.170
Right now, the application
is running on-premises.

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:38.280
Now, we'll go to Google Cloud
Platform, Google Kubernetes

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:39.710
Engine Cluster.

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:42.690
The database server has
already been migrated,

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:47.860
and a load balancer is already
configured for the application.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:51.420
Let's deploy Migrate for
Anthos into the cluster.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:54.780
After that's complete,
let's add the VMware setup

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.700
as a source for migration.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.940
Access to the remote setup
is facilitated by a Migrate

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.998
for Compute Engine deployment.

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:06.780
The setup is now complete.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:09.630
Let's create a migration to
generate the migration plan

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:11.540
YAML.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:14.180
And now, we can review
the migration plan YAML

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:15.800
in Cloud Shell.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.290
It's important to exclude
certain types of files

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:22.270
and directories
from the migration.

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:24.180
Now that we've reviewed
the migration plan,

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:27.780
we can start the artifact
generation process.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:32.190
For the application server,
this includes a container image,

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:37.630
a Docker file, and a
reference deployment YAML.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.560
Here, in Cloud
Shell, we can also

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:44.500
examine the migration
status of the application.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:48.140
Now, we'll return to our
VMware vSphere web client.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:52.050
A snapshot is automatically
taken on the VMware side.

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:54.870
This is to process the
migration without disruption

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:56.850
to the running workload.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:58.740
We can return to
Cloud Shell and see

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.460
when the migration is complete.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:03.910
Now, we'll return to the
Google Container Registry

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:05.750
and refresh the images.

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:08.210
When we look inside
the application folder,

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:10.600
we can see the captured
container image is now

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:14.240
ready in the Google
Container Registry.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.540
When we refresh the bucket
in Google container storage,

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:20.240
we can look inside and
see that the container

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.110
artifacts are stored in GCS.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:28.640
Now, we'll go back to Cloud
Shell to download the artifacts

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.170
and review the deployment YAML.

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:40.560
After inspecting
the deployment YAML,

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.725
we will apply the
deployment to Kubernetes

00:02:42.725 --> 00:02:43.600
to test the workload.

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:50.240
We'll go back to the Google
Kubernetes Engine cluster

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:52.310
to look at the application
deployment details.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:58.840
When we refresh the application,
we can see that all three

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.930
of the managed pods
within this application

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:05.920
are now running in a cluster
in Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:08.620
Finally, we'll go back
to our application, which

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.350
is no longer running
on-premises but is now

00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:14.380
running in containers in Google
Kubernetes Engine on Google

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:16.450
Cloud Platform.

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:18.600
[MUSIC PLAYING]

